problems.problem:
Find all pairs of functions $f, g: \mathbb R \to \mathbb R$ such that

(i) if $x < y$, then $f(x) < f(y)$;

(ii) $f(xy) = g(y)f(x) + f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb R$.

For positive numbers $a_1,a_2,\dots,a_n$ define $$A=\frac{a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n}{n}, \quad G=\sqrt[n]{a_1\cdots a_n}, \quad H=\frac{n}{a_1^{-1}+\cdots+a_n^{-1}}.$$ Prove that

(i) $\frac{A}{H}\leq -1+2\left(\frac{A}{G}\right)^n$ for $n$ even;

(ii) $\frac{A}{H}\leq -\frac{n-2}{n}+\frac{2(n-1)}{n}\left(\frac{A}{G}\right)^n$ for $n$ odd.

Given an odd prime $p$, find all functions $f:\mathbb Z \to \mathbb Z$ satisfying the following two conditions:

(i) $f(m)=f(n)$ for all $m,n \in \mathbb Z$ such that $m\equiv n\pmod p$;

(ii) $f(mn)=f(m)f(n)$ for all $m,n \in \mathbb Z$.

Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying both conditions:

(i) $f(x) \ge 0$ for all real $x$;

(ii) for all real numbers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ with $ab + bc + cd = 0$, $$f(a-b) + f(c-d) = f(a) + f(b+c) + f(d).$$

A city has at least $3$ subway stations, and trains run in both directions. Suppose there is a route in this city that passes through at least $L + 1$ stations without visiting any station twice. Prove that at least one of the following holds:

(i) there exist three distinct stations $A$, $B$, $C$ such that there is no route from $A$ to $B$ avoiding $C$;

(ii) there is a way to start at some station and return to it without visiting any station twice that passes through at least $\lceil\sqrt{2L}\,\rceil$ stations, where $\lceil x \rceil$ is the smallest integer not less than $x$.

Let $U$ be a set of $m$ triangles. Prove that there always exists a subset $W$ of $U$ satisfying both conditions:

(i) $W$ contains at least $0.45\,m^{4/5}$ triangles;

(ii) there are no six distinct points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, $F$ such that the six triangles $ABC$, $BCD$, $CDE$, $DEF$, $EFA$, $FAB$ all belong to $W$.

In a room, $2017$ boxes are arranged in a circle. A set of boxes is called harmonious if it contains at least $2$ boxes and, moving clockwise from each box of the set, the number of boxes passed before first reaching the next box of the set is $0$ or odd. $30$ students enter the room one after another; each chooses several boxes forming a harmonious set and puts a slip with his name into each chosen box. Suppose the set of all boxes containing $30$ slips is not harmonious. Prove that there exist students $A$, $B$ and boxes $a$, $b$ with both properties:

(i) $A$ chose $a$ but not $b$, and $B$ chose $b$ but not $a$;

(ii) the number of boxes other than $a$, $b$ passed when moving clockwise from $a$ to $b$ is not odd, and none of these boxes was chosen by $A$ or $B$.

There are $2020$ groups, each consisting of one boy and one girl, and every student belongs to exactly one group. Students shake hands in pairs so that all four conditions hold:

(i) no two students of the same sex shake hands;

(ii) the boy and the girl of the same group shake hands exactly once;

(iii) a boy and a girl from different groups shake hands at most once;

(iv) among the $4$ students of any two groups, the total number of handshakes is at least $3$.

Prove that one can choose $4038$ students and arrange them in a circle so that any two neighbors have shaken hands.

Let $k \ge 8$ be a positive integer. Prove that if there exist positive integers $x$, $y$ satisfying the conditions below, then there are infinitely many such ordered pairs $(x, y)$:

(1) $\dfrac{x^2-2}{y}$ and $\dfrac{y^2-3}{x}$ are positive integers;

(2) $\gcd\left(3x + \dfrac{2(y^2-3)}{x},\ 2y + \dfrac{3(x^2-2)}{y}\right) = k$.

A high school has $n \ge 2$ clubs $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n$. Prove that there exist $n - 1$ groups $B_1, B_2, \dots, B_{n-1}$ such that

(1) $A_1 \cup A_2 \cup \cdots \cup A_n = B_1 \cup B_2 \cup \cdots \cup B_{n-1}$;

(2) for $1 \le i < j \le n-1$, $B_i \cap B_j = \emptyset$ and $-1 \le |B_i| - |B_j| \le 1$, where $|X|$ is the number of elements of $X$;

(3) for each $1 \le i \le n-1$ there exist $1 \le k \le l \le n$ with $B_i \subseteq A_k \cup A_l$.

A set $X$ is called good if it satisfies all three conditions:

(i) $X$ has $2022$ elements;

(ii) each element of $X$ is a closed interval contained in $[0, 1]$;

(iii) for every real number $r$ with $0 \le r \le 1$, at most $1011$ elements of $X$ contain $r$.

For two good sets $A$ and $B$, let $n(A, B)$ be the number of ordered pairs $(I, J)$ with $I \in A$, $J \in B$ and $I \cap J \ne \emptyset$. Find the largest possible value of $n(A, B)$.

For a subset $T$ of $S = \{1, 2, \dots, 1000\}$, define $\widetilde{T} = \{1001 - t \mid t \in T\}$ (with $\widetilde{T} = \emptyset$ if $T$ is empty). Find the maximum number of elements of a family $\mathcal{P}$ satisfying all three conditions:

- every element of $\mathcal{P}$ is a subset of $S$;

- for any two elements $A$, $B$ of $\mathcal{P}$, $A \cap B$ is nonempty;

- for every element $A$ of $\mathcal{P}$, $\widetilde{A} \in \mathcal{P}$.

Prove that there is no quadruple $(p, q, r, n)$ of positive integers satisfying both of the following conditions:
- $p$, $q$, $r$ are primes;
- $p^n + q^n + r^n = 2026(p + q)(q + r)(r + p)$.
